cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "QuAsm: a quantum-simulator DSL interpreted by hand-written RISC-V assembly"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Keshav
    given-names: Shriharshith
    email: shriharshithkeshav@gmail.com
repository-code: "https://github.com/shriii1389/quasm"
license: MIT
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-11-08
keywords:
  - quantum computing
  - statevector simulation
  - RISC-V
  - assembly language
  - domain-specific language
  - differential testing
  - computer architecture education
abstract: >-
  QuAsm is a domain-specific language for statevector quantum simulation whose
  entire interpreter is hand-written RISC-V assembly, with no library
  underneath: the parser, the complex arithmetic, the symbol tables, the
  pseudorandom number generator, the depolarising noise channel and the control
  flow are all assembly, running under the RARS simulator. Programs declare
  registers of up to six qubits, apply single- and two-qubit gates including a
  controlled-phase family sufficient for the quantum Fourier transform, measure
  and collapse, branch on measurement outcomes, and loop. Correctness is
  established by differential testing against an independent NumPy oracle built
  deliberately on different algorithms, a static audit of the gate matrices,
  replay of observed measurement outcomes, cross-validation against Qiskit,
  metamorphic properties and grammar-based differential fuzzing, with mutation
  analysis measuring what each layer catches. The defects that effort found,
  and why behavioural tests missed them, are the substance of the accompanying
  paper.
